# Persian Typography, Natural Voice & RTL Standards

1. **Natural Voice (De-AI-ing):**
   - Avoid "می‌باشد"; use "است" or "هست".
   - Avoid robotic intros ("لازم به ذکر است", "شایان ذکر است", "در جهان پرشتاب امروزی").
   - Replace Arabic letters (`ي`, `ك`) with Persian ones (`ی`, `ک`).

2. **Chat & UI Output:**
   - In HTML-supported chat interfaces, encapsulate in `<div dir="rtl" style="text-align: right; direction: rtl; font-family: Tahoma, 'IRANYekanX', 'Vazirmatn', sans-serif;">`.
   - Never start a paragraph with Latin characters or numbers.
   - Wrap English words, variables, and code in backticks or `<span class="en-font" dir="ltr">...</span>` or `<bdi>`.
   - Accurate ZWNJ (نیم‌فاصله) on compound words and continuous verbs ("می‌شود", "پیاده‌سازی").
   - Attach punctuation (، ؟ . ؛) to the preceding word without space. Use Persian quotes (« ») and decimal separator (٫).

3. **Front-end & CSS Rules:**
   - NEVER use `letter-spacing` on Persian text.
   - Always set `dir="ltr"` on numeric/sensitive inputs (phone, card, IBAN, password, email).
   - Use CSS logical properties (`margin-inline-start`, `border-inline-start`).
   - In HTML docs, use Google Fonts Vazirmatn fallback.

4. **Office & Encoding:**
   - Save Persian CSV files with UTF-8 BOM (`utf-8-sig` in Python, `\uFEFF` in JS).
   - All source files UTF-8.
